
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Classic script global. One more question, then I'll write the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what happens when the page loads on a hostname that isn't in the map — a preview deploy, a new staging host,
  someone's LAN IP, a colleague's tunnel. This is a default, and defaults are what you live with during an incident.

  - [1mFail loudly[0m — no match means the settings module throws, and the login form doesn't submit. An unlisted host is a
    configuration bug, and it announces itself immediately instead of at the worst moment. Costs: a forgotten hostname
    takes the page down rather than degrading.
  - [1mFall back to development[0m — unknown hosts talk to the dev API. Never leaks real credentials or traffic to prod from
    an unrecognized host. Costs: a misconfigured [3mproduction[0m deploy silently points at dev and looks fine until someone
    notices logins aren't persisting.
  - [1mFall back to production[0m — unknown hosts talk to prod. The page always works. Costs: this is the one that sends real
    login traffic from a preview or local host to the production API without anyone deciding to — the failure mode I'd
    least want on a login endpoint.

  [1mMy recommendation: fail loudly.[0m On an auth endpoint, a wrong-environment default is worse than a broken page, and the
  map is three lines — a loud failure is fixed in under a minute. Silent fallbacks on login flows are how staging
  credentials end up in prod logs.

  Also, an assumption I need you to confirm or correct, since I can't read it from the repo: I'm planning three
  environments — [1mdevelopment[0m ([38;5;153mlocalhost[39m, [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m), [1mstaging[0m, [1mproduction[0m — with today's [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m as
  production, and the staging/dev URLs left as clearly-marked placeholders for you to fill in. Correct me if the set or
  the hostnames differ.
